{"seq": 1, "type": "user_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:31:27.000337+00:00", "payload": {"text": "# Your workspace\n\nYou are in `/work`, inside a Linux container with no network access. Write a Go program here.\n[Maelstrom](https://github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom) will start that program once for every node\nof a simulated cluster, send it client requests, and check that the answers are correct.\n\nWhat is installed:\n\n- Go 1.27.\n- Maelstrom 0.2.4, as the `maelstrom` command, so you can test your program yourself.\n- Maelstrom's Go library, already in the module cache. Start your module with:\n\n      go mod init node\n      go get github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go@v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d\n\nA node talks to the rest of the cluster through standard input and output: it reads one JSON\nmessage per line on stdin and writes one per line on stdout. Each message has a `src`, a `dest`\nand a `body`, and the body's `type` says what it is. Anything written to stderr is a log line.\nThe library does that framing for you: `maelstrom.NewNode()`, then `Handle` a message type,\n`Reply` to a request, `Send` or `RPC` to another node, and `Run` to start. It also answers the\n`init` message that tells each node its own ID and the IDs of all the nodes.\n\n# Ticket numbers\n\nWrite a node for a box office spread over five machines. Each machine issues tickets, and no two\ntickets may ever carry the same number, whichever machines issued them.\n\nA client asks any node to issue a ticket:\n\n    {\"type\": \"issue\", \"msg_id\": 7}\n\nand the node answers with the ticket it issued:\n\n    {\"type\": \"issued\", \"in_reply_to\": 7, \"ticket\": \"n3-204\"}\n\nA ticket can be any JSON value: a string, a number, an array. Clients ask for about a thousand\ntickets a second, spread over all five nodes, and from time to time the network splits the\nnodes into groups that can't reach each other. Every request must still be answered at once,\neven by a node that is cut off from all the others, and no ticket may repeat for the whole test.\n\n## How it's graded\n\nYou have 30 minutes and 100 steps, a step being one reply from you, however many tools it runs. If either runs out, you are stopped where you are.\n\nWhen you stop, your program is built with `go build -o node .` and tested 3 times with:\n\n    MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN=/work/node MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"id\": \"ticket\"}, \"types\": {\"generate\": \"issue\", \"generate_ok\": \"issued\"}}' maelstrom test --workload unique-ids --bin /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename --availability total --concurrency 2n --nemesis partition --node-count 5 --rate 1000 --time-limit 30\n\nThe runs are independent, each with its own timing and faults. The attempt passes only if it builds and every run is valid.\n\nMaelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. `maelstrom-rename` stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with the command above as it is.\n"}}
{"seq": 2, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:31:33.980292+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "We need to write a Go program that issues unique ticket numbers. The workload is \"unique-ids\" from Maelstrom, with the rename shim translating \"generate\" -> \"issue\" and \"generate_ok\" -> \"issued\", and the field \"id\" -> \"ticket\".\n\nThe unique-ids workload: clients send `generate` requests, nodes must reply with `generate_ok` containing a globally unique `id`. Availability is total, so even during partitions each node must respond immediately. The standard solution: each node returns a UUID, or a combination of node ID and a local counter.\n\nSimplest robust approach: generate a UUID per request (e.g., using a counter per node combined with node ID). Since node IDs are unique (n1..n5), `fmt.Sprintf(\"%s-%d\", nodeID, counter)` is unique forever. But careful: each node is started once per test run, so the counter starts at 0. But could a node restart within a run? Maelstrom's unique-ids workload doesn't kill/restart nodes unless nemesis includes process reboots... The nemesis is partition only. However, to be safe against restarts, we could use a random component too, or persist. Simpler: use UUID v4 style randomness plus node ID — guaranteed unique practically. But \"no ticket may repeat for the whole test\" — the checker checks uniqueness of all returned ids. Using a random UUID gives negligible collision probability but why risk? Use node ID + counter. Even if the process restarts, the counter resets... with partition nemesis only, no restarts happen. But to be extra safe, add a random session token generated at startup: `nodeID-counter-randomsuffix`. That makes it robust to restarts too (with negligible collision chance for the random part, 32 bits is fine; use 64-bit random).\n\nTicket can be any JSON value. Use a string like \"n3-204-abc123\". Actually simplest: `fmt.Sprintf(\"%s-%d-%x\", nodeID, counter, rand64)`.\n\nLet me write the program.\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"math/rand\"\n\t\"sync/atomic\"\n\n\tmaelstrom \"github.com/jepsen-io/maelstrom/demo/go\"\n)\n\nfunc main() {\n\tn := maelstrom.NewNode()\n\tvar counter uint64\n\t// random session id\n\tsession := rand.Uint64()\n\n\tn.Handle(\"issue\", func(msg maelstrom.Message) error {\n\t\tc := atomic.AddUint64(&counter, 1)\n\t\treturn n.Reply(msg, map[string]any{\n\t\t\t\"type\": \"issued\",\n\t\t\t\"ticket\": fmt.Sprintf(\"%s-%d-%x\", n.ID(), c, session),\n\t\t})\n\t})\n\n\tif err := n.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\tpanic(err)\n\t}\n}\n```\n\nWait — need to check: the maelstrom Go library `Reply` will include \"in_reply_to\" automatically. Yes, the library's Reply adds in_reply_to from msg_id.\n\nAlso, message body handling: The rename shim translates types and fields. The client sends {\"type\":\"generate\",\"msg_id\":7} -> shim sends {\"type\":\"issue\",\"msg_id\":7} to node. Node replies {\"type\":\"issued\",\"in_reply_to\":7,\"ticket\":\"...\"} -> shim translates to {\"type\":\"generate_ok\",\"in_reply_to\":7,\"id\":\"...\"}.\n\nActually wait: does maelstrom-rename rename fields in both directions? The config is `{\"fields\": {\"id\": \"ticket\"}, \"types\": {\"generate\": \"issue\", \"generate_ok\": \"issued\"}}`. The rename tool (maelstrom-rename) is a Clojure/Java program included with Maelstrom? Let me check — MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN points to /work/node and the bin is /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename. Let's check if that exists in the container. Probably it's a script that the test infra set up. Let me look.\n\nAlso need to handle potential \"issue\" messages without msg_id (broadcast-style)? No, clients use RPC.\n\nLet me also consider rate 1000/s across 5 nodes with concurrency 2n = 10. A simple counter with atomic is plenty fast.\n\nLet's set up the module, build, and test with the exact command.\n\nAlso note: go build with Go 1.27 — fine.\n\nOne concern: the maelstrom demo/go library version: v0.0.0-20260710121306-480a8197026d. It's already in the module cache. Need `go mod init node` and `go get ...`. No network access — go get may try network but module is in cache; use GOFLAGS=-mod=mod maybe. Let's just try.\n\nLet's start.\n\nAlso, stderr logs: the library logs to stderr? Keep minimal.\n\nLet me check the environment first.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "ls -la /work; go version; which maelstrom maelstrom-rename; ls /usr/local/bin"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2041941db7c20951a0f83", "name": "shell"}, {"arguments": {"command": "ls $(go env GOMODCACHE)/github.com/jepsen-io 2>/dev/null"}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2041942297661b84ceba6", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 1174, "output_tokens": 1087}}}
