{"seq": 34, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:32:05.227762+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "The shim is a Go program that reads MAELSTROM_RENAME and MAELSTROM_RENAME_BIN, spawns the child, and rewrites JSON messages both ways. Fine.\n\nNow write our program. Let me create go.mod and main.go.\n\nKey considerations:\n1. Handle \"issue\" → reply \"issued\" with \"ticket\".\n2. Uniqueness: nodeID + monotonic counter + random session token.\n3. Robustness: don't die on unknown message types. The maelstrom Go library returns error → exit. To be extra safe, I could register a fallback? The library has no fallback mechanism. Options: use the library and accept the risk, or write a tiny custom loop.\n\nGiven the grading stakes (every run must be valid), and simplicity, I'll write a custom main loop but still use the library for init handling? Hmm. Actually let's think: is there any message type the shim could forward that we don't handle? The shim forwards everything it doesn't handle itself. What does Maelstrom send to nodes besides init and workload RPCs? In the unique-ids workload, nothing else. However, Maelstrom's network layer sends... nothing at the protocol level. Some workloads send \"topology\" etc. Not here.\n\nBut there's another subtlety: the library's Run returns an error \"No handler\" only for non-reply messages without in_reply_to. If a client request times out and then retries, still \"issue\". Fine.\n\nRisk is low, but a custom loop eliminates it entirely and is trivial. However, the instructions suggest using the library. I'll use the library (it handles init and framing well), but I'll pre-register a set of handlers to reduce risk? I can't register a catch-all.\n\nAlternative: use the library but wrap: I could register handlers for common types (issue, plus \"generate\" just in case the env rename isn't applied in some path). That's cheap insurance: handle both \"issue\" and \"generate\" with the same function, replying with both \"ticket\" and \"id\"? No — if the shim renames field names, having both would be harmless? If the shim renames \"ticket\"→\"id\" and we send both, the client schema requires exactly type generate_ok with id s/Any — extra fields might be rejected by the schema (prismatic schema strict). c/defrpc with schema: it likely uses `s/validate` on the body; schema with s/eq keys... Actually maelstrom client's schema validation typically errors on extra keys? Prismatic Schema's map validation by default ignores extra keys? No — schema.core/validate on a map schema requires exact match of required keys but allows extra keys unless using `s/strict`. Hmm, maelstrom's c/defrpc might use strict. Risky. Better to keep the reply body minimal: {\"type\":\"issued\",\"ticket\":...}.\n\nActually, if the rename is applied to field names in both directions... Wait: does the shim rename \"id\"→\"ticket\" when sending to our node (i.e., the client→node direction), and \"ticket\"→\"id\" when receiving from our node? Since our node never receives a field named \"id\" (the generate request has only type and msg_id), it doesn't matter for requests. For replies, we emit \"ticket\", and the shim renames it to \"id\". Good.\n\nSo handle both \"issue\" and \"generate\" types (in case rename of types somehow doesn't apply) but reply with only... hmm, if we handle \"generate\" we'd reply with type \"issued\"? Then the shim would translate \"issued\"→\"generate_ok\" and \"ticket\"→\"id\"? Wait the mapping \"types\": {\"generate\": \"issue\", \"generate_ok\": \"issued\"} — direction: shim converts from maelstrom-side names to our-side names. So incoming \"generate\" becomes \"issue\"; outgoing \"issued\" becomes \"generate_ok\". If we reply with type \"generate_ok\" and field \"id\", the shim would try to map \"generate_ok\"→? The types map maps maelstrom names to program names, so reverse map would be applied... it would look up \"generate_ok\" in the reverse direction: program→maelstrom, so \"generate_ok\" 