{"seq": 64, "type": "assistant_message", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:34:29.568802+00:00", "payload": {"reasoning": "Everything is in place. Note the go.mod now shows the require without \"// indirect\" — go build tidy'd it? Actually `go get` marked it indirect and then building without -mod=mod rewrote go.mod (go 1.27 auto-fixes). Fine.\n\nDone. Summarize.", "text": "Done. The node is in `/work/main.go`, builds with `go build -o node .`, and passed three full Maelstrom runs.\n\n**Design:** each machine mints tickets on its own, with no coordination at all, so a partitioned or fully isolated node still answers instantly:\n\n- `ticket = \"<node-id>-<local counter>-<random session tag>\"`, e.g. `n3-204-668cdcddde7c87c9`.\n- The node ID comes from `init` and is unique per machine, so a local monotonic counter (`atomic.Uint64`) makes tickets globally unique with zero network traffic — uniqueness holds even while every node is cut off from every other.\n- A per-process 64-bit random session tag is folded in too, so a node that restarted (counter reset to zero) still couldn't re-issue an old ticket.\n- Handlers reply immediately to `issue` with `{\"type\":\"issued\",\"ticket\":...}` (and, as belt-and-braces, to Maelstrom's own `generate`/`generate_ok`/`id` names), and an `init` handler closes a \"ready\" gate so a ticket is never minted without the node's own ID. The library's chatty per-message stderr logging is silenced to keep up with 1000 req/s.\n\n**Results** (exact grading command, 3 runs): all `valid? true`, \"Everything looks good!\", ~27 900–28 000 ops per 30 s run, `ok-fraction 1.0`, `duplicated-count 0` under the partition nemesis.", "tool_calls": [], "usage": {"input_tokens": 29167, "output_tokens": 400}}}
